latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Calculate color frequencies for the entire grid.\n   b. Create a "uniqueness map" where each cell\'s value is based on how rare its color is in the overall grid.\n   c. Identify distinct patterns or structures (e.g., lines, clusters, shapes) using pattern recognition algorithms.\n\n2. Generate Candidate Regions:\n   a. Use a sliding window approach with varying sizes (from 3x3 up to 10x10).\n   b. For each window, calculate:\n      - Color diversity (number of unique colors)\n      - Pattern diversity (variety of local patterns)\n      - Uniqueness score (based on the uniqueness map)\n      - Representativeness score (how well it reflects the overall grid\'s color distribution)\n\n3. Create Signature Subgrids:\n   a. For each candidate region, create a "signature" subgrid:\n      - If the region is already compact and diverse, use it as is.\n      - If it\'s larger, try to compress it by removing less significant parts while maintaining its essence.\n   b. Ensure each signature subgrid is between 3x3 and 10x10 in size.\n\n4. Score Signature Subgrids:\n   a. Develop a scoring function that considers:\n      - How well the subgrid represents the input grid\'s overall character\n      - The subgrid\'s internal color and pattern diversity\n      - The uniqueness of the colors and patterns within the subgrid\n      - The compactness of the subgrid (favoring smaller sizes that still capture the essence)\n   b. Apply this scoring function to each signature subgrid.\n\n5. Select the Best Signature Subgrid:\n   a. Choose the signature subgrid with the highest score.\n   b. In case of ties, prefer the smaller subgrid.\n\n6. Refine the Selected Subgrid:\n   a. Adjust the boundaries slightly if it improves the representation of the input grid\'s essence.\n   b. Ensure any adjustments keep the subgrid within the 3x3 to 10x10 size constraints.\n\n7. Post-process the Output:\n   a. Remove any all-zero rows or columns from the edges, if present.\n   b. If removal makes the subgrid smaller than 3x3, expand it to include adjacent cells from the original region.\n\n8. Validate the Final Output:\n   a. Confirm the subgrid meets the size constraints (3x3 to 10x10).\n   b. Verify that it captures a representative "signature" of the input grid.\n\n9. Return the Final Subgrid:\n   a. Convert the selected and refined subgrid into the required ColoredGrid format.\n   b. Return this as the solution.\n\nThis plan aims to be flexible enough to handle the variety of cases seen in the examples, focusing on capturing a representative "essence" of the input grid rather than maximizing specific attributes like size or density. It allows for different sizes and types of outputs depending on what best represents the unique characteristics of each input grid.'
